<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Greenpeace</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1971">1971</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Greenpeace is an international environmental organisation that grew out of a 1971 protest voyage from Vancouver against American nuclear tests in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaska">Alaska</a>. It became known for direct action at sea against nuclear testing, whaling and pollution. In 1985 French intelligence agents sank its ship Rainbow Warrior in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auckland">Auckland</a> harbour, killing the photographer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fernando-pereira">Fernando Pereira</a>, an attack that strengthened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>'s anti-nuclear stance.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/greenpeace.jpg" alt="Greenpeace" width="320" height="142" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Salto Loco · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Greenpeace_offices_world_map.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In the late 1960s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> planned its Cannikin underground nuclear test on Amchitka, a tectonically unstable island in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaska">Alaska</a>, and there were concerns that it might trigger earthquakes and a tsunami. About 7,000 people blocked the Peace Arch border crossing between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-columbia">British Columbia</a> and Washington, carrying signs reading Don't Make A Wave. The protests did not stop the detonation.</p><p>When the Amchitka tests were over, the young organisation turned to French atmospheric nuclear testing at Moruroa Atoll in French Polynesia. In 1972 David McTaggart, a former businessman living in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>, renamed his 12.5-metre ketch Vega as Greenpeace III and sailed it into the exclusion zone, a voyage sponsored by the New Zealand branch of the Campaign for Nuclear Disarmament. The French Navy tried to stop it and assaulted McTaggart; a crew member photographed the incident and made it public, and after the publicity <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> announced it would stop atmospheric tests.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Greenpeace evolved from a group of Canadian and American protesters into a less conservative group of environmentalists, reflecting the counterculture of the 1960s and 1970s. Seagoing ships have been central to its campaigns. In 1978 it launched the first Rainbow Warrior, a 40-metre former fishing trawler first used to disrupt the Icelandic whaling fleet. Between 1978 and 1985 its crews also acted against the ocean dumping of toxic and radioactive waste, the grey seal hunt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a> and nuclear testing in the Pacific. In May 1985 it carried out Operation Exodus, evacuating about 300 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rongelap">Rongelap Atoll</a> islanders whose home had been contaminated by fallout from a US nuclear test.</p><p>In the 1990s the organisation was influential in raising public awareness of global warming and, with the refrigerator industry, developed a CFC-free refrigerator technology called Greenfreeze; the United Nations Environment Programme gave it an award in 1997 for contributions to protecting the ozone layer. In 2007 six activists who climbed the smokestack of the Kingsnorth power station in Kent were acquitted after arguing that they had a lawful excuse to prevent greater damage from climate change.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Greenpeace is a campaigning network of 26 independent national and regional organisations, with a coordinating body, Greenpeace International, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>. It does not accept funding from governments, corporations or political parties, relying on individual supporters and foundation grants. It is known for nonviolent direct action and has been criticised, among other things over its opposition to genetically modified organisms, which led 107 Nobel laureates to sign an open letter in June 2016, and over damage to rock near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazca-lines">Nazca Lines</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> in December 2014.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Greenpeace” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>On 10 July 1985 French intelligence agents sank the <b>Greenpeace</b> ship Rainbow Warrior in Auckland harbour, killing the photographer Fernando Pereira.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-zealand">New Zealand · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Eric Baker was a founder of Amnesty International and of the Campaign for Nuclear Disarmament, Edith Pye established the Famine Relief Committee that led to Oxfam, and Irving and Dorothy Stowe co-founded <b>Greenpeace</b> in 1971.</blockquote><a href="/movement/quakers">Quakers · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>In December 2014 <b>Greenpeace</b> activists set up a banner within the lines of one geoglyph, damaging the site; Greenpeace apologised, and one activist was convicted and fined.</blockquote><a href="/work/nazca-lines">Nazca Lines · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li></ul></section>
